test_crush_thin_tets -- how crush_thin_tets moves a tet mesh from poor
quality to better, and why some poor tets are left (user, Oct 21 2026:
"I'd like to see some quality metrics so I can see how the algorithm
moves from poor quality to better quality, and also identify why some
poor quality cells remain"; "examples that use the 3D Poisson Disk code")
=======================================================================

QUICK START

  sh run_crush_tests.sh [path-to-lagrit]     13 decks, about 15 s
  open logs/summary.html

  Default lagrit: build/lagrit of this LaGriT checkout.

WORDS

  Poor tets are SLIVER, WEDGE, NEEDLE and CAP (the user's words). Two
  ways of sorting them are reported side by side:

  Carl's classes (ex_id_tet_types; LaGriT computes the attributes in
  ct_measure.mlgi, the checker applies the thresholds):
    SLIVER  min dihedral <= 10, max dihedral >= 170, max solid angle <= 10
    CAP     max solid angle >= 170
    NEEDLE  edge ratio (shortest / longest) <= 0.1, min dihedral > 10
    WEDGE   edge ratio <= 0.1, min dihedral <= 10
    poor    any dihedral <= 10 or >= 170       (angles in degrees)

  crush's view: crush_thin_tets works on every tet whose RELATIVE WIDTH
  (smallest altitude / sqrt(sum of squared edge lengths / 9); 1 regular,
  0 flat) is below TOLCRUSH (default 0.1), and sorts it by what comes
  close, in this order: NEEDLE (two vertices: a short edge, merged;
  tested at 1.2 x TOLCRUSH), WEDGE (a vertex near an edge: a point added
  on the edge, the vertex merged onto it; 1.1 x TOLCRUSH), CAP (a vertex
  near the opposite face: a point added on the face, the vertex merged
  onto it or it into the vertex; switched off before Oct 2026), SLIVER
  (two opposite edges nearly crossing: a point added on each, the two
  merged). A thin tet that matches none is UNMATCHED. With a pset crush
  works only on tets whose vertices are all in the pset or were added by
  crush itself.
  crush acts only if the worst tet around the change gets at least 1%
  better, and repeats in trips (at most 10) until a trip changes nothing.

THE DECKS

  Planted (make_inputs.py): a regular lattice tet mesh (6 x 6 x 6
  vertices, 750 tets, relative width 0.41 everywhere) with one vertex
  moved to make one thin tet of a chosen kind; every tet stays valid.
  ct01-ct04  NEEDLE, WEDGE, CAP, SLIVER inside the mesh
  ct05-ct08  the same on the bottom boundary face (for CAP: an interior
             vertex just above a boundary face)
  ct09       every lattice vertex moved at random (thin tets of all kinds)
  3D Poisson disk (createpts / poisson_disk / 3d_box / ... / connect):
  ct10       unit cube, h = 0.1        (839 vertices, 3,780 tets)
  ct11       unit cube, h graded 0.05 - 0.15 from a vertical line
             (ct_h_field.mlgi; 447 vertices, 1,959 tets)
  ct13       unit cube, h = 0.05           (5,630 vertices, 30,080 tets)
  ct10, ct11 and ct13 are crushed at TOLCRUSH 0.1, 0.2 and 0.3, each from the
  same mesh.
  ct12       the ct10 mesh crushed at 0.3 with a pset (vertices below
             z = 0.5): every tet with no vertex in the pset must be left
             as it was

  Moving one vertex to make a CAP always leaves another thin tet beside
  it (a sliver); crush collapses that and the cap goes with it. A cap on
  its own cannot be planted this way; the Poisson meshes show real ones.

WHAT IS MEASURED (check_crush.py; definitions in its opening comment)

  Before and after: vertices, tets, minimum relative width and the
  number of tets below 0.05 / 0.1 / 0.2 / 0.3, LaGriT's aspect ratio,
  min and max dihedral angle, Carl's classes, crush's view. Trip by
  trip: the quality report crush prints at the start of each trip
  (aspect ratio counts, tets) and the vertices merged in each trip.
  Pictures: the tets below relative width 0.3, before and after.

  Every tet still below TOLCRUSH afterwards is explained by replaying
  crush's own decisions on the final mesh (crushlib.Mesh.replay, the
  same steps as find_00, find_01, find_02, find_11 in
  src/crush_thin_tets.f): every possible collapse leaves a neighbour no
  better (not 1% better); boundary or material point types forbid the
  merge; matched no case (no distance below TOLCRUSH x scale length);
  outside the pset; and, for a LaGriT from before Oct 2026, CAP case
  switched off. The checker picks the version of crush (the replay
  models the current one and the older ones) under which crush would
  do nothing more, since crush stops only then.

  PASS / FAIL: LaGriT finished; every tet has positive volume; the total
  volume is unchanged (1e-9); for "expect: removed", no tet is left
  below TOLCRUSH; with "# pset:", no tet outside the pset changed.
  Everything else is measured, not judged.

FOUND (Oct 21-22 2026)

  Tets left below TOLCRUSH 0.1 / 0.2 / 0.3 (ct13 h = 0.05, ct10 h = 0.1):

                         ct13            ct10
    before the fixes     3 / 36 / 301    0 / 1 / 27
    1. scale fixed       0 / 24 / 242    0 / 2 / 36
    2. + CAP case on     0 / 15 / 188    0 / 1 / 32
    3. + ladder (now)    0 / 15 / 164    0 / 1 / 25
  ct11 (graded): 0 / 0 / 8 before the fixes, 0 / 0 / 11 now (crush
  adds 4% - 13% vertices; the 11 are 6 not-1%-better and 5 caps).

  NOTE: until Oct 22 2026 ct_h_field.mlgi had four command lines longer
  than 80 columns. LaGriT reads 80 columns of a line, so the rest was
  cut off ("Illegal option or arguments to math") and the h field stayed
  uniform: the deck then called "ct11 graded" was uniform h = 0.05. That
  mesh is now ct13 (the numbers above are for it); ct11 is truly graded.
  The checker now fails a deck that prints "Illegal option".

  1. BUG, FIXED: the length every distance test is compared with
     (TOLCRUSH x scale length) was the scale length of mesh vertices 1-4
     for EVERY tet, not of the tet itself (local vertex numbers 1-4 used
     as global ones). In ct13 it was 0.044 for tets near 0.07: real
     slivers were passed over ("matched no case": 3 of 3 left at 0.1,
     197 of 301 at 0.3). In ct10 it happened to be larger than the tets
     (0.13), so crush acted more freely there.
  2. FIXED: the CAP case was switched off (if (.false.) around find_02;
     the face refinement it needs was half disabled). find_02 is
     rewritten: the vertex nearest its opposite face (foot inside the
     face, distance below TOLCRUSH x scale length) gets a point on that
     face, and is merged onto it (or it into the vertex) if that makes
     the worst tet around 1% better. Caps were the largest group left
     (ct13 at 0.3: 169 of 242); now 80 of 164.
  3. BUG, FIXED: len_iadd_tr was never set, so a second crush_thin_tets
     in one run could write past the end of iadd_tr (a crash in a
     Release build once the CAP case is on).
  4. FIXED: the looser tolerances for NEEDLE (1.2 x) and WEDGE (1.1 x)
     documented in the code were commented out; restored (ct13 at 0.3:
     188 to 164; ct10: 32 to 25; nothing worse).
  5. BUG, FIXED: the pset was ignored (its test commented out): crush
     changed tets anywhere (ct12 on the old code: 253 tets outside the
     pset changed). Now only tets whose vertices are all in the pset or
     were added by crush.
  6. What is left is the 1% rule: every tet still below TOLCRUSH is one
     where each possible collapse would leave a neighbour no better
     (ct13 at 0.3: 164 tets, 80 of them caps whose face merge would not
     help; ct10: 25, 6 of them caps; the boundary sliver ct08).
  7. crush MAKES caps: its edge splits and merges create tets with one
     vertex just over a face. Carl's CAP count in ct13 goes from 0 to
     56 at 0.2 and 31 at 0.3 (65 and 80 before the CAP case was on).
  8. TOLCRUSH 0.1 (the default) is weaker than a 10 degree dihedral
     rule: relative width 0.1 is about 5 degrees. ct13: Carl's "poor"
     283 before, 185 after 0.1, 6 after 0.2, 2 after 0.3.
  9. crush adds vertices (ct13: +2% at 0.1, +16% at 0.3) and never
     smooths or reconnects (recon is commented out); maxtrip is fixed at
     10 (the most any deck here used is 9: ct13 at 0.3).

  The angle attributes this suite uses (ang_mind, ang_maxd, solid
  angles) were themselves checked in ../test_element_angles.

FILES

  run_crush_tests.sh   runner (work/, logs/; WORK= and LOGS= may change them)
  make_inputs.py       planted and jittered lattice meshes
  crushlib.py          measures, classification, crush replay
  check_crush.py       checker; writes logs/<tag>.json and .check
  make_page.py         logs/summary.html
  ct_measure.mlgi      Carl's attributes on a copy of the mesh
  ct_h_field.mlgi      the graded h field for ct11
  ct01-ct13 *.lgi      the decks
